From: Jacob Root <me@rooty.dev>
Subject: [PATCH DRAFT] KVM: x86: KVM_PRE_FAULT_MEMORY_WRITE, a prefault that maps
 for write

NOT APPLIED ANYWHERE. A draft for the host kernel, against Linux 7.2.7;
patches/linux/README.md says what has and has not been done with it.

KVM_PRE_FAULT_MEMORY maps memory as a stage-2 read fault would. A read
fault maps a page writable only when GUP-fast can prove the host mapping
writable (virt/kvm/kvm_main.c hva_to_pfn_fast(), then the FOLL_WRITE
retry in hva_to_pfn_slow()), and with CONFIG_SECRETMEM GUP-fast refuses
every order-0 folio whose ->mapping is NULL (mm/gup.c
gup_fast_folio_allowed()) -- which is every page a driver hands userspace
with vm_insert_page(), such as the NVIDIA driver's system memory
(kernel-open/nvidia/nv-mmap.c nvidia_mmap_sysmem()). A VMM that places
such a mapping in guest memory and prefaults it gets it mapped read-only,
and the guest's first write to each page takes a second, slow stage-2
fault: a guest writing freshly allocated GPU staging memory writes it at
1 GB/s where the host writes it at 20.

A write-intent flag lets the VMM ask for the mapping the guest's own write
would have made: writable wherever the host mapping is writable (the slow
GUP path takes FOLL_WRITE and succeeds for these pages), breaking CoW and
dirtying the page in the dirty log as a guest write would. Userspace
probes for it: an unknown flag is EINVAL, as every flag is today.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_0119BJfiAjA3VRAtdz3uU35x
---
diff --git a/arch/x86/include/asm/kvm_host.h b/arch/x86/include/asm/kvm_host.h
--- a/arch/x86/include/asm/kvm_host.h
+++ b/arch/x86/include/asm/kvm_host.h
@@ -475,6 +475,8 @@
 
 #define KVM_HAVE_MMU_RWLOCK
 
+#define KVM_ARCH_PRE_FAULT_MEMORY_FLAGS	KVM_PRE_FAULT_MEMORY_WRITE
+
 struct kvm_mmu_page;
 struct kvm_page_fault;
 
diff --git a/arch/x86/kvm/mmu/mmu.c b/arch/x86/kvm/mmu/mmu.c
--- a/arch/x86/kvm/mmu/mmu.c
+++ b/arch/x86/kvm/mmu/mmu.c
@@ -5073,6 +5073,22 @@
 		direct_bits = gfn_to_gpa(kvm_gfn_direct_bits(vcpu->kvm));
 
 	/*
+	 * A write fault maps the page writable if the host mapping allows it,
+	 * and with it breaks CoW and dirties the page in the dirty log, as a
+	 * guest write would.  A read fault maps a page writable only when
+	 * GUP-fast can prove it writable (hva_to_pfn_fast()), and GUP-fast
+	 * refuses some pages outright -- with CONFIG_SECRETMEM, every order-0
+	 * folio without a mapping, which is what a driver's vm_insert_page()
+	 * gives userspace -- so the first guest write to each such page would
+	 * take a second fault.  Private memory is prefaulted as it is.
+	 */
+	if (range->flags & KVM_PRE_FAULT_MEMORY_WRITE) {
+		if (error_code & PFERR_PRIVATE_ACCESS)
+			return -EINVAL;
+		error_code |= PFERR_WRITE_MASK;
+	}
+
+	/*
 	 * Shadow paging uses GVA for kvm page fault, so restrict to
 	 * two-dimensional paging.
 	 */
diff --git a/Documentation/virt/kvm/api.rst b/Documentation/virt/kvm/api.rst
--- a/Documentation/virt/kvm/api.rst
+++ b/Documentation/virt/kvm/api.rst
@@ -6529,7 +6529,15 @@
 will fail with `EOPNOTSUPP` even if `KVM_CHECK_EXTENSION` reports
 the capability to be present.
 
-`flags` must currently be zero.
+`flags` is zero, or on x86 KVM_PRE_FAULT_MEMORY_WRITE: memory is then
+mapped as if the vCPU had generated a stage-2 *write* fault -- writable
+wherever the host mapping is writable, breaking CoW and marking the pages
+dirty in the dirty log, as a guest write would.  Without it a page that the
+host could map writable may still be mapped read-only, e.g. one GUP-fast
+cannot check, and the guest's first write to it then faults again.  A
+read-only memslot fails with ENOENT, and private memory with EINVAL.
+Another flag, or KVM_PRE_FAULT_MEMORY_WRITE on another architecture, fails
+with EINVAL, so a VMM can probe for it.
 
 4.144 KVM_S390_KEYOP
 --------------------
diff --git a/include/linux/kvm_host.h b/include/linux/kvm_host.h
--- a/include/linux/kvm_host.h
+++ b/include/linux/kvm_host.h
@@ -2614,6 +2614,10 @@
 #ifdef CONFIG_KVM_GENERIC_PRE_FAULT_MEMORY
 long kvm_arch_vcpu_pre_fault_memory(struct kvm_vcpu *vcpu,
 				    struct kvm_pre_fault_memory *range);
+/* The KVM_PRE_FAULT_MEMORY_* flags an architecture accepts. */
+#ifndef KVM_ARCH_PRE_FAULT_MEMORY_FLAGS
+#define KVM_ARCH_PRE_FAULT_MEMORY_FLAGS	0
+#endif
 #endif
 
 #endif
diff --git a/include/uapi/linux/kvm.h b/include/uapi/linux/kvm.h
--- a/include/uapi/linux/kvm.h
+++ b/include/uapi/linux/kvm.h
@@ -1670,4 +1670,7 @@
 	__u64 padding[5];
 };
 
+/* Map as a write fault would: writable where the host mapping allows it. */
+#define KVM_PRE_FAULT_MEMORY_WRITE	(1ULL << 0)
+
 #endif /* __LINUX_KVM_H */
diff --git a/virt/kvm/kvm_main.c b/virt/kvm/kvm_main.c
--- a/virt/kvm/kvm_main.c
+++ b/virt/kvm/kvm_main.c
@@ -4338,7 +4338,7 @@
 	long r;
 	u64 full_size;
 
-	if (range->flags)
+	if (range->flags & ~KVM_ARCH_PRE_FAULT_MEMORY_FLAGS)
 		return -EINVAL;
 
 	if (!PAGE_ALIGNED(range->gpa) ||
